<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amenemhat II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">20th–19th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> · Also known as Amenemhet II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amenemhat II was the third king of the Twelfth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning for more than thirty years around the turn of the twentieth and nineteenth centuries BCE. His reign was largely peaceful and prosperous, with expeditions to Sinai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> and contacts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> recorded in an annals inscription from Memphis. He was buried in a pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amenemhat-ii.jpg" alt="Amenemhat II" width="320" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan R. Lazaro · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amenemhat_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amenemhat II was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> and of Neferu III, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a>, who is named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-sinuhe">Story of Sinuhe</a> as the wife of Senusret I. He had two sisters, Sebat and Itakayet, who were buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisht">Lisht</a> near the pyramid of Senusret I. The identities of his spouses are unknown. An early mention may come from the tomb of the nomarch Amenemhat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beni-hasan">Beni Hasan</a>, who escorted the king's son Ameny on an expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>; this prince is believed to be Amenemhat II in his youth. On taking the throne he took the prenomen Nubkaure, the name found mostly on private monuments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A double-dated stela of an official named Wepwawet-aa, dated to Year 44 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> and Year 2 of Amenemhat II, has sometimes been read as evidence of a coregency, while other researchers regard that as unlikely. The most important record of his early reign is the so-called Annals of Amenemhat II, found as fragments at Memphis and later reused in the 19th Dynasty. It records donations to temples and some political events, among them a military expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, the destruction of two cities, Iuai and Iasy, whose locations are unknown, and the coming of tribute-bearers from Asia and Kush.</p><p>Several mining expeditions are known: at least three in the Sinai, one in the Wadi Gasus in Year 28, and one for amethysts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-el-hudi">Wadi el-Hudi</a>. He ordered building works at Heliopolis, Herakleopolis, Memphis and the Eastern Delta and rebuilt a ruined temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a>. The Great Sphinx of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanis">Tanis</a>, later usurped by many kings, is associated with him. A treasure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> objects found under the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montu">Montu</a> at Tod bears his name, and many of its objects are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> rather than Egyptian, which shows contacts between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and other civilisations. His highest dated attestation is Year 35, on the stela of Hapu at Konosso, which also shows that he shared a brief coregency with his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-ii">Senusret II</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His mummy has not been recovered. He chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dahshur">Dahshur</a> for his pyramid, originally named Amenu-sekhem and now called the White Pyramid, a site not used as a royal cemetery since the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a>. His two predecessors had built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisht">Lisht</a>. The pyramid is poorly preserved and only partly excavated.</p><p>Jacques Morgan discovered tombs in the complex in 1894 or 1895. They included the burials of three women titled king's daughters, Ita, Itaweret and Khenmet, found untouched with their jewels and weapons; examination of their remains shows that the women used the weapons regularly. His court is known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> such as Senusret and Ameny, from the treasurers Rehuerdjersen and Merykau, and from the provincial governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khnumhotep-ii">Khnumhotep II</a>, whose decorated tomb is at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beni-hasan">Beni Hasan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amenemhat II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
